{"lines":[{"t":"Reading","h":0.019339236831215745,"c":1,"x":0.79123420863765737,"w":0.080274219512939449,"y":0.096148837600278259},{"t":"The study team used neuroimaging equipment to contrast the brain activity of silbadores while","w":0.75514404296874993,"h":0.01598837238037687,"x":0.088477361127372756,"y":0.14244186031923112,"c":1},{"t":"listening to whistled and spoken Spanish. Results showed the left temporal lobe of the brain,","h":0.01949512495168948,"c":1,"y":0.15640578469604616,"w":0.7388510131835937,"x":0.086341415076664138},{"c":1,"t":"which is usually associated with spoken language, was engaged during the processing of Silbo.","y":0.17579505306651733,"w":0.76131688435872391,"h":0.016195524146054185,"x":0.086419747013981876},{"c":1,"x":0.088477380116705334,"t":"The researchers found that other key regions in the brain's frontal lobe also responded to the","w":0.73456787109374999,"y":0.19040697693107012,"h":0.017484660423827014},{"c":1,"w":0.75102879842122405,"h":0.01598837238037687,"t":"whistles, including those activated in response to sign language among deaf people. When the","x":0.086419757056928004,"y":0.20930232543527383},{"y":0.22526501738769944,"c":1,"x":0.086419764272536378,"t":"experiments were repeated with non-whistlers, however, activation was observed in all areas of","h":0.017467540902720735,"w":0.75925923665364581},{"y":0.24162444335804012,"h":0.014455604328564409,"t":"the brain.","w":0.083318748474121088,"c":1,"x":0.086068737179218355},{"t":"'Our results provide more evidence about the flexibility of human capacity for language in a","x":0.086352989845210767,"w":0.73674031575520826,"c":1,"y":0.27468798996815158,"h":0.017633631316455789},{"c":1,"x":0.086419762482417936,"y":0.29063604246351571,"w":0.74074071248372397,"t":"variety of forms,' Corina says.iThese data suggest that left-hemisphere language regions are","h":0.017503491840879293},{"y":0.30948174313346577,"c":1,"t":"uniquely adapted for communicative purposes, independent of the modality of signal. The non-","h":0.016195524146054074,"w":0.76131688435872391,"x":0.08641974701160203},{"y":0.32353629032443298,"h":0.019589780216643926,"t":"Silbo speakers were not recognising Silbo as a language. They had nothing to grab onto, so","c":1,"w":0.72856206258138023,"x":0.08633324655556654},{"x":0.086419752838276284,"w":0.36213991800944006,"h":0.015988372380376759,"c":1,"y":0.34156976729312416,"t":"multiple areas of their brains were activated.'"},{"h":0.018091425878841494,"w":0.7820039876302084,"y":0.37497161311506921,"t":"Carreiras says the origins of Silbo Gomero remain obscure, but that indigenous Canary Islanders,","x":0.086371681478926335,"c":1},{"t":"who were of North African origin, already had a whistled language when Spain conquered the","h":0.01598837238037687,"c":1,"w":0.75308639526367183,"y":0.39244186031873873,"x":0.086419765208782445},{"h":0.018194203101563611,"y":0.40771001821497932,"c":1,"w":0.74707112630208328,"t":"volcanic islands in the 15th century. Whistled languages survive today in Papua New Guinea,","x":0.086352242211429106},{"x":0.086419747797054358,"y":0.42579505306321153,"w":0.75514404296874993,"h":0.016195524146054185,"t":"Mexico, Vietnam, Guyana, China, Nepal, Senegal, and a few mountainous pockets in southern","c":1},{"y":0.44183181618136613,"x":0.086360841759657206,"h":0.017740698950310385,"c":1,"t":"Europe. There are thought to be as many as 70 whistled languages still in use, though only 12","w":0.74701889038085934},{"w":0.73479482014973962,"h":0.019184005836154316,"c":1,"y":0.45636837828496413,"t":"have been described and studied scientifically. This form of communication is an adaptation","x":0.086305287885912685},{"y":0.47526914552690214,"c":1,"x":0.086357496017085589,"t":"found among cultures where people are often isolated from each other, according to Julien","w":0.72439071655273435,"h":0.017817037827274951},{"t":"Meyer, a researcher at the Institute of Human Sciences in Lyon, France. 'They are mostly used in","x":0.086419753264295213,"y":0.49263839809956345,"w":0.77572016398111976,"h":0.016195524146054185,"c":1},{"t":"mountains or dense forests,' he says.ồạiWhistled languages are quite clearly defined and represent","c":1,"y":0.50757685023552546,"x":0.086335255764780072,"h":0.019614054541705728,"w":0.76971720377604169},{"x":0.08636724843256588,"h":0.018993418124033767,"y":0.52468127593895852,"t":"an original adaptation of the spoken language for the needs of isolated human groups?'","c":1,"w":0.69354064941406257},{"x":0.086419750785771049,"w":0.73662551879882809,"y":0.55948174313365007,"h":0.016195524146054185,"c":1,"t":"But with modern communication technology now widely available, researchers say whistled"},{"w":0.78395060221354163,"h":0.015996932421752708,"c":1,"t":"languages like Silbo are threatened with extinction. With dwindling numbers of Gomera islanders","x":0.084362147191210213,"y":0.57558139519979523},{"c":1,"t":"still fluent in the language, Canaries' authorities are taking steps to try to ensure its survival.","h":0.017441859655301539,"w":0.73456787109374999,"x":0.086419766802233372,"y":0.591569767701419},{"w":0.76543207804361979,"y":0.60895170800243226,"x":0.086419762582193013,"c":1,"h":0.017501780506578801,"t":"Since 1999, Silbo Gomero has been taught in all of the island's elementary schools. In addition,"},{"w":0.72222223917643225,"h":0.016195524146054185,"y":0.62632508816713206,"c":1,"x":0.084362131337709728,"t":"locals are seeking assistance from the United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural"},{"x":0.086354923902199801,"t":"Organization (UNESCO).iẠạỊThe local authorities are trying to get an award from the organisation","y":0.64182874587036154,"w":0.77174173990885431,"c":1,"h":0.018827084517731629},{"h":0.017450419696677599,"c":1,"t":"to declare [Silbo Gomero] as something that should be preserved for humanity, Carreiras adds.","x":0.084362155216873208,"y":0.65842167274725227,"w":0.76543207804361979},{"c":1,"h":0.014534883982160851,"y":0.91715116251473339,"t":"85","x":0.84362139882469511,"w":0.026748971939086985}],"w":2400,"h":3395}